
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

The corrected acquisition code also carries compile-time assertions for:

- 32-byte V1/V2 packet size and 25-byte payload;
- `DATA_STATUS` ECG-ready mask `0xE0`;
- target `ADCMAX=0xF30000` and zero code `0x798000`;
- target native ODR integer component for R1=4/R2=6/R3=16;
- R1/R2/R3 register-value decoding and supported `REVID=0x01`;
- latest-only DRDY collapse, exact native sequence advancement, and diagnostic
  byte saturation without wrap;
- conservative discard of a read whose event identity becomes ambiguous due
  to a post-claim DRDY.

The native host fixture runs the production `ECGPacket.cpp` and `CRC16.cpp`
without hardware and compares the encoded packet against an independently
specified byte array, including the known CRC `0x8926`:

```bash
pio test -e native_packet_test
```

All four current firmware environments must compile as the minimum regression
gate. Hardware-only behavior (register readback, DRDY count, internal self-test,
and packet-loss soak testing) cannot be claimed from compile tests alone.
